---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Tombstone from '../components/Tombstone.astro';
import { path } from '../lib/paths';
import { LEIDEN, LICENCE, SITE } from '../lib/site';

/** The practice schema, in submission order. Mirrors CLAUDE.md's content model. When the
 *  form is built this list moves to src/lib/ and both read from it. */
const fields = [
  'A title, and whether the work was research, learning, teaching, or writing',
  'The task — literature search, conjecture generation, proof drafting, proof checking, formalisation, computation, exposition, translation, referee work',
  'Which tools, at which version, on which date',
  'What you were trying to do, and then what you actually did, step by step',
  'The outcome: worked, partially worked, or did not work',
  'How you verified correctness',
  'An excerpt of the transcript',
  'Caveats, and what you would do differently',
  'Tags, so the account can be found by subject',
  'Time spent, whether the work was published, whether the AI use was disclosed in the paper, and how confident you are in the result',
];
---

<Base title={SITE.name} description={SITE.description} bareTitle>
  <div class="shell">
    {/* ── Statement ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
    <section class="statement" aria-labelledby="statement-heading">
      <h1 id="statement-heading">MathemAct</h1>
      <p class="mission">{SITE.mission}</p>

      <div class="statement__body prose">
        <p class="lead">
          MathemAct collects first-hand accounts of how mathematicians use AI tools in
          their work: what you set out to do, what you actually did, what came out, and
          how you checked that the result was correct.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every account follows the same structure, so that
          it can be compared, filtered, counted, and cited. This amounts to a corpus of detailed knowledge rather than a series of impressions shared in passing among colleagues.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* ── Principles and practice ──────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="principles-heading">
      <h2 id="principles-heading">Principles, and the practice underneath them</h2>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          In June 2026 the <a href={LEIDEN.url}>{LEIDEN.name}</a> asked researchers to
          <q>{LEIDEN.quotes.disclosure}</q>. It also conceded something useful:
          <q>{LEIDEN.quotes.evolving}</q>.
        </p>
        <p>
          That concession is where this site works. A principle can say that disclosure
          should happen. It cannot say what a disclosure should contain when the tool was
          used across four sessions over six hours, produced two usable lemmas and one
          confident falsehood, and the error was caught only because someone thought to
          check a boundary case.
        </p>
        <p>
          Nobody can specify that in the abstract. It has to be read off real cases. So
          MathemAct accumulates them in a fixed schema, until the schema is good enough
          that a journal could hand it to authors as a disclosure template.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="disclaimer">
        MathemAct is an independent project. It is not affiliated with the Leiden
        Declaration, its working group, or the International Mathematical Union, and it
        carries no endorsement from any of them.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* ── What a contribution looks like ───────────────────────────────────────── */}
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="contribution-heading">
      <h2 id="contribution-heading">What a contribution looks like</h2>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          An account is called a practice: one piece of work, reported by the person who
          did it. The form asks for these, in this order.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ul class="tombstone-list prose">
        {fields.map((field) => <li>{field}</li>)}
      </ul>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          One of them has no skip and no <q>not applicable</q>.
          <strong>How you verified correctness</strong> is required on every account, without
          exception. It is the field that separates a corpus a mathematician can use from a
          pile of anecdotes. There are plenty of places to put anecdotes; this is not one.
        </p>
        <p>
          The transcript excerpt is pasted in and stored here rather than linked. Share
          links expire, get revoked, and sometimes breach the provider's terms. The
          excerpt is the record. You confirm, before posting, that you have removed
          anyone else's unpublished material from it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Writing one should take under ten minutes. If it takes longer, the form is
          wrong, not you.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* ── Three things worth knowing ───────────────────────────────────────────── */}
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="different-heading">
      <h2 id="different-heading">Three things worth knowing</h2>

      <div class="points">
        <article class="point">
          <h3>Failures count the same as successes</h3>
          <div class="point__body">
            <p>
              An account reporting that the tool got it wrong is worth as much here as one
              reporting that it got it right, and often more, because failure modes are
              what nobody publishes. A corpus of successes alone would be advertising.
              Nothing in the form or the interface treats a failure as the lesser
              contribution.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="point">
          <h3>Every practice has an expiry</h3>
          <div class="point__body">
            <p>
              Model, version, and date of use are required, and any signed-in
              mathematician can record that a practice still works or no longer does. A
              method that held against a 2025 model may be nonsense against a 2026 one.
              The square beside each account says which it is:
            </p>
            <ul class="legend" role="list">
              <li><Tombstone status="verified" /> — a correctness check is recorded, and someone has confirmed it still works</li>
              <li><Tombstone status="stale" /> — not rechecked since an older model</li>
              <li><Tombstone status="broken" /> — someone tried it and reported that it failed</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="point">
          <h3>The whole corpus is downloadable</h3>
          <div class="point__body">
            <p>
              Everything published here is exported nightly as JSON and licensed
              <a href={LICENCE.url} rel="license">{LICENCE.name}</a>, so it can be analysed
              without scraping and without asking. Researchers studying how mathematics
              adopts these tools are among the people this is built for.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* ── Call to action ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
    <section class="cta" aria-labelledby="cta-heading">
      <h2 id="cta-heading" class="cta__heading">Read how this works</h2>
      <p class="cta__body">
        The about page has the full picture: the schema, how a verified email becomes an
        institutional badge, and what is and is not kept about you.
      </p>
      <p><a class="button" href={path('/about/')}>How this works</a></p>
      <p class="cta__note">
        Posting opens once accounts and moderation are in place.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Section rhythm is set here, once, rather than on each section's own class, so a new
     section inherits it without anyone remembering to. Element selectors inside a
     section are wrapped in :where() to keep them at zero specificity — a component
     dropped into a section must never have to out-specify the page. */
  .statement {
    padding-block: var(--space-2xl) var(--space-xl);
  }

  .section {
    padding-block: var(--space-xl);
    border-block-start: var(--border);
  }

  .statement :where(h1) {
    font-size: var(--size-8);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .mission {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-block-start: var(--space-xs);
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: var(--size-4);
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .statement__body {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-lg);
  }

  .lead {
    font-size: var(--size-4);
    line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
  }

  .section :where(h2) {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-block-end: var(--space-md);
  }

  /* Set apart from the surrounding prose because it is a legal statement, not an
     argument, and it must not read as part of the case being made. */
  .disclaimer {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-block-start: var(--space-lg);
    padding-inline-start: var(--space-sm);
    border-inline-start: var(--border-width-thick) solid var(--rule);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--size-2);
  }

  .tombstone-list {
    margin-block: var(--space-md);
  }

  /* Three columns on wide screens, one on narrow. auto-fit rather than a media query, so
     the breakpoint follows the content width instead of a guessed device size. */
  .points {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(17rem, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-lg);
    margin-block-start: var(--space-md);
  }

  /* Subgrid so the headings share a row and the bodies share the next one. Without it a
     heading that wraps to two lines pushes its own paragraph down and the three columns
     start at three different heights, which is exactly the kind of raggedness this
     audience reads as carelessness. */
  .point {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-rows: subgrid;
    grid-row: span 2;
    row-gap: var(--space-2xs);
  }

  .point :where(h3) {
    font-size: var(--size-4);
  }

  .legend {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-sm);
    padding: 0;
    font-size: var(--size-2);
  }

  /* Normal inline flow, not flex. Tombstone's root is already inline-flex, so it sits on
     the text baseline unaided — whereas making the li a flex container turns the trailing
     description into one unbreakable flex item, which wraps onto its own line and reads
     as a separate legend entry. */
  .legend > li + li {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-3xs);
  }

  /* ── Call to action ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .cta {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-xl);
    padding: var(--space-lg);
    border: var(--border-strong);
    background-color: var(--surface);
  }

  .cta__heading {
    font-size: var(--size-5);
  }

  .cta__body {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-block: var(--space-2xs) var(--space-md);
  }

  .button {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: var(--space-2xs) var(--space-md);
    border: var(--border-strong);
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .button:hover {
    background-color: var(--ink);
    color: var(--ground);
  }

  .cta__note {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-md);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--size-2);
  }

  @media (max-width: 30rem) {
    .cta {
      padding: var(--space-md);
    }
  }
</style>
